// NESTED_IF_WORKAROUND (dispatch): calls wp_mc_assign rather than inlining it.
// Revert scope for the in-flight compiler fix — grep NESTED_IF_WORKAROUND. See CLAUDE.md section 2.
// wp_mem_controller_step — mem_controller's per-event handler, and the ONLY
// place in this whole slice that talks to disk_controller. Never touches a
// block's content — only its own status-cell bookkeeping. Every group of
// related values travels as ONE list-valued message on ONE channel.
//
// 1. Waits for the seal signal, which now CARRIES the stream and the sealed
//    length. Both used to be derivable here — there was one stream, and a
//    block was always seq * payload capacity of an undelimited input. Under
//    F1v a block ends wherever the next record would not fit, so its length is
//    a fact only the packer holds; under S2 the stream decides which directory
//    the flusher writes it to. Deriving either downstream is exactly the kind
//    of second source of truth that can disagree after a crash.
// 2. Recalls, via its own self-mailbox, which block address + status cell it
//    most recently assigned, and forwards
//    [ptr, len, seq, status, stream] to disk_controller. This is the sole
//    synchronization point in the pipeline: nothing is forwarded for ejection
//    before the seal signal confirms the write actually finished.
// 3. Takes the next free (ptr, status) pair off the pool — blocking here until
//    flusher has reclaimed one, which is the real backpressure a pool of
//    exactly 3 forces — marks it 0(not in use)->1(writing), plans ITS footer,
//    and hands the bundle to hot_mem_write as the next block to write into.
//
// The footer records a SEQUENCE NUMBER, never a raw memory pointer: a pointer
// from mem_reserve_raw is meaningful only within one process lifetime, and
// with a pool of 3 physical blocks the same pointer is reused across many
// unrelated blocks. -1 is the "no continuation, this is the last block"
// sentinel. Sequence numbers are now ABSOLUTE — B1 starts this run wherever
// the durable store left off — so the plan compares against the run's last id
// rather than against a count from zero. Under S2 that id is global across
// both streams, which is what keeps the union dense and lets a structure
// record name a payload block by a single integer.
// AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH step 5a: state is "<seq> FS <done>" and the bound
// is an end-of-stream sentinel rather than a predicted block count. The
// sentinel (stream = -1) cannot be confused with a seal: a real one carries a
// stream selector of 0 or 1. Both `if`s below are top-level, and the live half
// is a fn CALL rather than an inline block — an `if` nested inside a branch
// executes its untaken side (see wp_flusher_step).
fn wp_mem_controller_step(state: Text) -> Text {
  let fs     = chr(Int(28))
  let seq    = str_to_int(str_before(state, fs))

  let sig    = bchan_take(Text("wp-seal-done"))
  let stream = list_get(sig, Int(0))
  let len    = list_get(sig, Int(1))

  let eos    = int_lt(stream, Int(0))
  let _fwd   = if eos {
                 bchan_send(Text("wp-dc"), list_of_1(Int(2)))
               } else {
                 io_print(Text(""))
               }
  let out    = if eos {
                 str_concat(int_to_str(seq), str_concat(fs, Text("1")))
               } else {
                 wp_mc_assign(state, stream, len)
               }
  str_concat(out, Text(""))
}
